# tests/lookaround/lookbehind.rxt -- module `lookaround` ([M6.6.2]): `(?<=` and `(?<!`: fixed bodies, SAME-length alternatives (design §10.2)
#
# GENERATED BY tests/lookaround/gen_corpus.py, and that is a property rather
# than a convenience. Every expectation below was produced by driving the cell
# through libpcre2 10.46 (the committed ctypes binding at
# docs/design/eng_brep_measurements/probes/pcre2_ctypes.py) BEFORE it was
# written, and python3 `re` was driven over the SAME cells in the same pass; a
# block carries `# pcre2-only` exactly where python diverged or could not
# compile the pattern -- DETECTED, never assumed, with the first divergence and
# the cell count recorded above the marking. The generator never asks pcrec
# anything: an expectation derived from the compiler under test is not an
# expectation.
#
# WHY THE MARKING IS COMPUTED RATHER THAN DECLARED. Design §7 catalogues TWO
# expectations about this module that a hand-marking would have written in and
# that are REFUTED by measurement: python compiles all fourteen QUANTIFIED
# lookaround forms and agrees on all nine behavioural cells (G8), and the two
# oracles agree on all 27 CAPTURE cells including captures in a negative
# lookahead (G9). R32 C3 is the standing precedent -- a test plan that marked
# two files python-verifiable in the direction that LOSES the oracle.
#
# EVERY BLOCK NAMES `lookaround` IN ITS `features` LINE (R33 V-10): `std1` is a
# FROZEN named set, {classes, modifiers}, so it does not contain this module,
# and a corpus cell that forgot the feature would pass by REFUSAL -- S108's
# masking shape applied to a whole file.
#
# Design: docs/design/lookaround_design.md.


# THE SHAPE ITSELF, and the second subject is design §3.4's B5 by name:
# `(?<=a)b` on "b" is NOMATCH because the START-OF-SUBJECT GUARD
# (`scan_position < k`) fires before the back-step is ever called. Its
# negative twin below answers (0,1) on the same subject, which is what makes
# the guard's ANSWER observable rather than just its existence.
pattern (?<=a)b
features lookaround
m "ab" 1 2
n "b"
n "xb"
n "a"
m "aab" 2 3

# A MULTI-CHARACTER fixed body. "bcx" is B5's second cell: two characters
# precede the `x` and the branch needs three.
pattern (?<=abc)x
features lookaround
m "abcx" 3 4
n "bcx"
n "x"
m "abcxy" 3 4

# CLASSES behind the cursor. Width 2, fixed, and the body is emitted by
# `vm_emit` unchanged -- a class test inside a lookbehind is the same
# one-line class test it is anywhere else (§3.5(3)).
pattern (?<=[ab][cd])x
features lookaround
m "acx" 2 3
m "bdx" 2 3
n "aax"
n "cx"

# AN EXACT COUNT IS FIXED WIDTH, and it is the cell §3.4's follow ruling
# turns on: `a{3}` is lowered as an `A_REP` that takes a cursor rung with an
# MRL clamp whose literal MOVES with the follow, so a lookbehind whose body
# was left unscoped would get a different bound here. F3 measured exactly
# this pair.
pattern (?<=a{3})x
features lookaround
m "aaax" 3 4
n "aax"
m "aaaax" 4 5

# ...and the SHARPER half of that pair (R33's verifier's own cell): an exact
# count over a GROUP is fixed width too, and a reader is likelier to think a
# group is not a rung than to think `a{3}` is not.
pattern (?<=(?:ab){2})x
features lookaround
m "ababx" 4 5
n "abx"
n "ababab"

# TWO BRANCHES OF THE SAME WIDTH -- the alternation cell python ACCEPTS
# (G10). Each branch gets its own back-step and its own retry frame; branch 1
# failing retreats into branch 2 through the ordinary fail label.
pattern (?<=ab|cd)x
features lookaround
m "abx" 2 3
m "cdx" 2 3
n "aex"
n "bx"
n "x"

# THREE same-width branches, so the chain is longer than the two-branch shape
# a reader would generalise from: the LAST branch pushes no retry frame at
# all, and only a third branch shows that the middle one does.
pattern (?<=ab|cd|ef)x
features lookaround
m "abx" 2 3
m "cdx" 2 3
m "efx" 2 3
n "ghx"

# THE NEGATIVE FORM, and "b" is B5's discriminating subject: (0,1) here where
# the positive form is NOMATCH. This is also the polarity on which a wrong
# width would be a FALSE MATCH rather than a decline, which is why §3.4's
# end-check returns HARD on this arm (ASK 2).
pattern (?<!a)b
features lookaround
n "ab"
m "b" 0 1
m "xb" 1 2
m "bb" 0 1

# The negative form with same-width branches: the assertion holds only when
# EVERY branch fails, which is what running out of branches means -- ordinary
# failure into the pushed `L_neg_ok` frame (§3.3).
pattern (?<!ab|cd)x
features lookaround
n "abx"
n "cdx"
m "aex" 2 3
m "x" 0 1

# §2.6's DEGENERATE BODY, behind. An empty body always succeeds, so the
# positive form is a NO-OP -- and it needs no special case: the body is an
# A_EMPTY of width 0, ONE branch, and the back-step steps back zero
# characters.
pattern (?<=)x
features lookaround
m "x" 0 1
m "ax" 1 2
n "y"

# ...and the negative form with an empty body is `(*FAIL)`.
pattern (?<!)x
features lookaround
n "x"
n "ax"

# A lookbehind in the MIDDLE, where the entry position is not the attempt
# position -- the back-step is relative to the CURSOR, and the end-check is
# what proves the body landed back on it.
pattern a(?<=a)b
features lookaround
m "ab" 0 2
n "ac"
m "xab" 1 3

# CAPTURES INSIDE A LOOKBEHIND, retained on success exactly as a lookahead's
# are (§3.5(1) measures this cell: g1=(0,1) g2=(1,2)). It is also the first
# reason the body cannot be a reverse DFA: the reverse pass is over the
# CAPTURE-ERASED pattern (D31) and none of this survives erasure.
pattern (?<=(a)(b))c
features lookaround
m "abc" 2 3
g 1 0 1
g 2 1 2
n "axc"

# BRANCH ORDER AT EQUAL WIDTHS, which is the half of §2.4 level 1 that python
# can also verify: written order decides, and WHICH branch committed is
# observable in the groups. The differing-width half is in
# lookbehind_widths.rxt and is `# pcre2-only`.
pattern (?<=(aa)|(ab))c
features lookaround
m "aac" 2 3
g 1 0 2
g 2 -1 -1
m "abc" 2 3
g 1 -1 -1
g 2 0 2
n "bac"

# A lookbehind as the WHOLE pattern: a zero-width match AFTER an `a`, so the
# reported span is empty and its position is the assertion's.
pattern (?<=a)
features lookaround
m "a" 1 1
m "ba" 2 2
n "b"
n ""

# A NESTED LOOKAHEAD INSIDE A LOOKBEHIND, and it is width 1 -- a nested
# lookaround contributes 0 to both `minw` and `maxw` (§3.1(d)), so the branch
# stays FIXED and ships. It is also §3.2.1's uniform-scoping case: the inner
# assertion's cursor runs AHEAD of the entry position, so the lookbehind's
# own follow arithmetic does not hold inside it and the scoping has to be
# unconditional.
pattern (?<=a(?=b))ab
features lookaround
n "ab"
n "ac"
n "xab"

# A LOOKBEHIND INSIDE A LOOKBEHIND -- two position slots live at once, and
# the outer end-check must compare against the OUTER entry.
pattern (?<=(?<=a)b)c
features lookaround
m "abc" 2 3
n "xbc"
n "bc"

# A nested NEGATIVE lookahead inside a lookbehind: still width 1.
pattern (?<=a(?!b))x
features lookaround
m "ax" 1 2
n "abx"
n "x"

# One lookbehind per ALTERNATION BRANCH, so the two allocate different slots
# and neither's end-check may read the other's.
pattern (?<=a)b|(?<=bc)d
features lookaround
m "abd" 1 2
m "bcd" 2 3
m "ab" 1 2
n "xcd"

# A lookbehind inside a CAPTURE inside a QUANTIFIER: the assertion is
# re-entered per iteration and its slots are re-set on every entry.
pattern ((?<=a)b)+
features lookaround
m "ab" 1 2
g 1 1 2
m "abb" 1 2
g 1 1 2
n "b"
m "abab" 1 2
g 1 1 2

# A QUANTIFIED lookbehind, so the EMPTY-ITERATION guard is exercised behind
# as well as ahead: `vm_nullable` answers true for A_LOOK whatever its
# direction, and if it did not this cell would burn the step budget rather
# than hang (S127).
pattern (?<=a)*b
features lookaround
m "ab" 1 2
m "b" 0 1

# A lookbehind inside an ATOMIC GROUP: two cut marks live at once, and the
# outer cut must not discard frames the inner one still owns.
pattern (?>(?<=a)b)
features lookaround,atomic-groups
m "ab" 1 2
n "b"

# ...and the other nesting order, an atomic group inside a lookbehind. Fixed
# width 2, because the cut removes MATCHES and never BYTES.
pattern (?<=(?>ab))c
features lookaround,atomic-groups
m "abc" 2 3
n "bc"

# TWO LOOKBEHINDS IN SEQUENCE at the same position, of DIFFERENT depths --
# design §2.3's B4 composite (`PCRE2_INFO_MAXLOOKBEHIND` is the MAX, not the
# sum). Independent slots, independent end-checks.
pattern (?<=a)(?<=ab)c
features lookaround
n "abc"
n "xbc"
n "bc"

# ...and the same pair written deepest-first.
pattern (?<=ab)(?<=b)c
features lookaround
m "abc" 2 3
n "bc"

# THE ASSERTION FAMILY's OWN BODY. `\b` expands to
# `(?:(?<!\w)(?=\w)|(?<=\w)(?!\w))` and this is half of it -- one class,
# fixed width 1. §2.5's rule is exactly big enough for the whole family and
# that is MEASURED rather than arranged (§6.1).
pattern (?<=\w)x
features lookaround,classes
m "ax" 1 2
n "x"
n ".x"
m "9x" 1 2

# ...and the other half.
pattern (?<!\w)x
features lookaround,classes
n "ax"
m "x" 0 1
m ".x" 1 2

# The third body the family uses, and the one `(?m)^`'s expansion needs.
pattern (?<=\n)x
features lookaround
m "\nx" 1 2
n "x"
n "ax"

# A NEGATED class behind the cursor.
pattern (?<=[^a])b
features lookaround
m "xb" 1 2
n "ab"
n "b"

# An exact count of 2, the smallest body where the guard and the back-step
# disagree about how much they each rule out.
pattern (?<=a{2})b
features lookaround
m "aab" 2 3
n "ab"
m "aaab" 3 4

# ...negated, where a wrong width would be a FALSE MATCH.
pattern (?<!a{2})b
features lookaround
n "aab"
m "ab" 1 2
m "b" 0 1

# §2.7's FOUR COMPILING CELLS, the lookbehind member. The refusal is about
# `\K` INSIDE the assertion; once it has closed, `\K` is the ordinary
# construct module `assertions` already ships. A check that latched on "a
# lookaround was seen" would wrongly refuse this.
# python DIVERGES here, measured rather than assumed: 'ab' @0 pcre2=('m', 1,
# 2) python=('ERR', 'bad escape \\K at position 6') (2 cells in this block)
# pcre2-only
pattern (?<=a)\Kb
features assertions,lookaround
m "ab" 1 2
n "b"

# `\G` INSIDE A LOOKBEHIND means what it means outside one -- an absolute
# position test against `startpos` -- and needs nothing from this module
# (§3.8). The startpos axis for it is in startpos.rxt.
# python DIVERGES here, measured rather than assumed: 'ab' @0 pcre2=('m', 1,
# 2) python=('ERR', 'bad escape \\G at position 4') (2 cells in this block)
# pcre2-only
pattern (?<=\Ga)b
features assertions,lookaround
m "ab" 1 2
n "xab"
